.TH SHTOP 1 "2026-10-04" "shtop 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.SH NAME
shtop \- a calm, readable system monitor for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shtop
.RB [ \-i
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-animation ]
.RB [ \-\-icons | \-\-no\-icons ]
.RB [ \-\-dump
.IR COLS x ROWS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shtop
shows CPU, memory, network, storage, battery and temperatures, and every
running app, on one screen. A status line at the top says in plain words how
the system is doing, for example
\fI\(lqAll calm \- nothing is working hard\(rq\fR
or
\fI\(lqMemory is almost full \- chromium holds 2.1 GB\(rq\fR.
.PP
Every process is listed with its PID; press
.B g
to group them by app, so a browser with twenty processes is one row.
Press
.B ?
inside shtop for a guide to every panel and key.
.PP
shtop reads
.I /proc
and
.I /sys
directly and needs nothing beyond Python 3.11 or newer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fISECONDS\fR
How often processes and drives refresh, from 0.5 to 8 seconds (default 2).
CPU, memory and network graphs sample twice as often.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-apps
Start with processes grouped by app instead of listed one by one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-animation
Update numbers instantly instead of easing them. Uses less CPU.
.TP
.B \-\-icons
Use Nerd Font icons. This is the default when fontconfig knows a Nerd Font.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-icons
Use plain symbols that every monospace font has.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump " COLS" x ROWS
Print a single frame of that size to standard output and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR \(ua " " \(da ", " j " " k
Move the selection.
.B PgUp PgDn Home End
jump.
.TP
.BR Enter ", click"
Show details for the selected app or process.
.TP
.B /
Search by name, command line or PID.
.B Esc
clears the search.
.TP
.B g
Switch between individual processes and apps (processes grouped by name,
shown with the PID of the main process).
.TP
.BR s ", " c ", " m ", " n ", " p ", " r
Next sort; sort by CPU, memory, name or PID; reverse the sort.
Clicking a column header also sorts.
.TP
.BR Del ", " x
End the selected app nicely (SIGTERM), after confirming.
.TP
.BR Shift+Del ", " X
Force-end it (SIGKILL), after confirming.
.TP
.B t
Show or hide kernel tasks.
.TP
.B a
Turn animations on or off.
.TP
.BR + " " \-
Update faster or slower.
.TP
.BR ? ", " h ", " F1
Show the guide.
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl+C
Quit.
.SH COLOURS
shtop uses the first of these that exists:
.IP 1. 4
the file named by
.BR SHTOP_THEME ;
.IP 2. 4
.IR ~/.config/shtop/colors.toml ;
.IP 3. 4
the current Omarchy theme,
.IR ~/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme/colors.toml ,
which is followed live as you switch themes;
.IP 4. 4
the terminal's own palette, asked for at startup with OSC 4/10/11 queries;
.IP 5. 4
a built-in palette.
.PP
A colours file uses the Omarchy
.I colors.toml
format: keys such as
.BR background ,
.BR foreground ,
.BR accent ,
.BR muted ,
.BR selection ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.B cyan
and
.BR orange ,
each set to a
.RI \(dq# rrggbb \(dq
string. Missing keys fall back to the built-in palette. Files are re-read when
they change.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHTOP_THEME
Path to a colours file that takes priority over all others.
.TP
.B SHTOP_ICONS
.B 1
or
.B 0
to force icons on or off when neither
.B \-\-icons
nor
.B \-\-no\-icons
is given.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where to look for
.I shtop/colors.toml
(default
.IR ~/.config ).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Re-read the colours file now instead of within half a second.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR btop (1),
.BR htop (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR proc (5)
.SH AUTHOR
Shota Giorgobiani.
Source and bug reports: https://github.com/giorgobiani/shtop
